Affordable AI applications for Nigerian SMEs
Small and medium-sized businesses often face a different challenge from large enterprises. They need useful results without expensive infrastructure, large technical teams, or lengthy implementation projects.

Many SMEs should begin with existing business tools and a limited number of clearly defined workflows.
Business need	Practical starting point	How to measure value
Customer enquiries	AI-assisted replies using approved business information	Response time and enquiry resolution
Marketing	Drafting product descriptions and campaign variations	Qualified leads and conversion rate
Sales reporting	AI-assisted spreadsheet analysis	Time required to prepare reports and accuracy
Administration	Drafting letters, quotations, and routine documents	Staff hours saved and error rates
Customer follow-ups	Organised reminders and approved message drafts	Follow-up completion and repeat sales
Inventory	Better sales records and demand analysis	Stockouts, waste, and inventory costs
Staff knowledge	Searchable procedures and internal document summaries	Time needed to find accurate information
Bookkeeping support	Transaction categorisation and anomaly checks	Reconciliation time and error rates

A business should not automate every activity at once. Start with a task that happens frequently, consumes measurable staff time, and has a clear method for checking the output.

For example, a small distributor might begin by analysing sales records to identify its ten fastest-selling products. If the data is accurate, the resulting analysis might improve purchasing decisions without requiring a custom AI system.

A retail business might begin with AI-assisted product descriptions and customer enquiry drafts. Staff review every response before sending it. The owner then compares response times, customer engagement, and sales outcomes over a defined period.

The guiding principle is simple: pay for a solution when its expected business value exceeds its full cost and risk.
How to calculate return on investment
AI projects need a financial test. A tool that saves time but costs more than the value of the time saved might not be worthwhile.

A practical assessment includes five steps.
1. Establish a baseline. Record current processing time, staff hours, error rates, operating costs, or sales performance before introducing the tool.
2. Calculate the full cost. Include subscriptions, implementation, integration, training, maintenance, data preparation, security, and human review.
3. Measure the change. Compare the same business process after implementation. Account for seasonal changes, staff changes, and other factors that affect results.
4. Calculate the financial benefit. Estimate verified labour savings, reduced waste, fewer errors, additional contribution from sales, or other measurable gains.
5. Review the result. Continue, modify, or stop the project based on measured performance and risk.
